---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../styles/mex.scss";
import annotations from "../../assets/images/rki/annotations.png";
import improvements from "../../assets/images/rki/improvements.png";
import landingPage from "../../assets/images/rki/landingPage.png";
import mexCarriedContext from "../../assets/images/rki/mexCarriedContext.png";
import mexSelectedDatasets from "../../assets/images/rki/mexSelectedDatasets.png";
import missingData from "../../assets/images/rki/missingData.png";
import recordsRelationship from "../../assets/images/rki/recordsRelationship.png";
import settings from "../../assets/images/rki/settings.png";
import {Image} from "astro:assets";

const project = {
    client: "MEx · Robert Koch Institute",
    role: "Product designer & project manager",
    contributions:
        "Research · UX/UI design · Client facilitation · Front-end development · Prioritisation and delivery",
    team: "Four-person Cottage Labs team working with the RKI client team",
    period: "2025 – present",
    status: "Implemented · ongoing development and maintenance",
};
---

<BaseLayout
        title="MEx Metadata Explorer"
        description="Designing a metadata catalogue that helps researchers navigate more than 130,000 interconnected records."
>
    <main class="mex-case-study">
        <article class="case-study">
            <header class="project-hero">
                <p class="project-hero__client">{project.client}</p>

                <h1>
                    Helping researchers find the right data across more than 130,000 interconnected records
                </h1>

                <p class="project-hero__summary">
                    MEx is a metadata catalogue for the Robert Koch Institute, Germany’s national public health
                    institute. It helps researchers discover relevant datasets, variables, projects and
                    publications—including data that cannot be made publicly available.
                </p>

                <dl class="project-meta">
                    <div>
                        <dt>Role</dt>
                        <dd>{project.role}</dd>
                    </div>

                    <div>
                        <dt>Period</dt>
                        <dd>{project.period}</dd>
                    </div>

                    <div>
                        <dt>Status</dt>
                        <dd>{project.status}</dd>
                    </div>

                    <div>
                        <dt>Contributions</dt>
                        <dd>{project.contributions}</dd>
                    </div>

                    <div>
                        <dt>Team</dt>
                        <dd>{project.team}</dd>
                    </div>
                </dl>
            </header>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>Making complex metadata navigable</h2>

                <p>
                    Before researchers request access to a dataset, they need to know whether it could answer their
                    question. Because much public-health data cannot be made openly available, they must make that
                    judgement from its metadata: what was collected, which populations and time periods it covers, which
                    variables it contains and who can provide access.
                </p>

                <p>
                    At MEx’s scale, this becomes a substantial information-architecture challenge. More than 130,000
                    multilingual records connect datasets with variables, projects, publications, people and
                    organisational units. Those relationships provide valuable context, but each one also creates
                    another route through which researchers could lose track of their investigation.
                </p>

                <p>
                    As product designer and project manager, I shaped this experience from discovery through delivery. I
                    interviewed users, worked with RKI’s metadata experts to clarify requirements and priorities,
                    designed the UX and UI, coordinated the four-person Cottage Labs team and contributed to the
                    front-end implementation.
                </p>

                <figure class="case-study-figure hero">
                    <Image src={mexSelectedDatasets} format="webp" quality={82}
                           alt=""
                           class=""/>
                    <figcaption>
                        Caption placeholder: Introduce the search interface and explain that the prototype uses
                        fictional data.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>Understanding how researchers work</h2>

                <p>
                    RKI came to the project with detailed requirements and deep knowledge of its metadata. I interviewed
                    the client team’s metadata expert and team lead—both users of MEx—to understand how researchers
                    assess whether a dataset is worth pursuing and what information they need along the way.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Their workflow is not linear. Researchers begin with a broad search for potentially relevant
                    datasets, then inspect and compare the variables those datasets contain. What they learn during
                    comparison helps them exclude weaker candidates and informs the next dataset search.
                </p>

                <p>
                    We considered combining dataset and variable exploration within one search. This would create a
                    busier interface and restrict the specialised filters available for each record type. The better
                    solution is two separate searches—a familiar pattern for researchers—which allows Data Sources
                    &amp; Datasets Search to support discovery and Variables Search to support detailed comparison.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The trade-off is continuity. Researchers need to move repeatedly between the two searches without
                    losing track of the datasets they have already considered. Only after narrowing the selection to
                    the most suitable dataset do they contact the responsible unit to request access.
                </p>

                <figure class="case-study-figure research-journey">
                    <!-- todo: Recreate from the approved journey concept in line with the portfolio style -->
                    <div class="image-placeholder" aria-hidden="true">
                        Image placeholder: Simplified journey showing the loop from dataset discovery to variable
                        comparison and back.
                    </div>
                    <figcaption>
                        Caption placeholder: Describe the iterative research journey and identify the visual as a
                        simplified reconstruction created for this case study.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>Carrying context between two searches</h2>

                <p>
                    Keeping the searches separate protects the clarity of each interface but creates a continuity
                    problem. To bridge them, I proposed and designed <strong>Selected Datasets</strong>: a shared
                    selection carried between both contexts.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Researchers can gather promising datasets during discovery, use them to focus Variables Search, and
                    return to refine their selection without starting again. The feature preserves the thread of the
                    investigation as they narrow a broad set of possibilities into a focused selection worth pursuing.
                </p>

                <figure class="case-study-figure selected-datasets">
                    <!-- Suggested sources: 9a04995c-09ae-420b-8ce2-4341fe192287.png and 8d4aa0c6-1d3d-49e1-b9b6-e9123aa0255b.png -->
                    <Image src={mexCarriedContext} format="webp" quality={82}
                           alt=""
                           class=""/>
                    <div class="image-placeholder" aria-hidden="true">
                        Image placeholder: Focused crops of Data Sources &amp; Datasets Search and Variables Search,
                        showing that Selected Datasets persists between them.
                    </div>
                    <figcaption>
                        Caption placeholder: Explain how Selected Datasets carries the investigation between the two
                        specialised searches.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>Making dense metadata readable</h2>

                <p>
                    Presenting MEx’s metadata as a flat reflection of the data model would make it difficult to
                    understand what matters. I designed reusable cards that group information by meaning and use
                    human-friendly labels. Long lists and multi-value fields initially show a short preview, with the
                    full content available through <strong>Show more</strong>.
                </p>

                <figure class="case-study-figure metadata-cards">
                    <!-- Suggested sources: 41d9d3a3-0dd5-4db8-88ae-b419d1535a96.png and 635ad2c7-4c32-461e-bcda-a10e4f5c6e1f.png -->
                    <Image src={missingData} format="webp" quality={82}
                           alt=""
                           class=""/>
                    <div class="image-placeholder" aria-hidden="true">
                        Image placeholder: Focused comparison of a record with missing metadata hidden and revealed.
                    </div>
                    <figcaption>
                        Caption placeholder: Explain how the interface keeps the default view manageable while allowing
                        researchers to inspect meaningful gaps in the metadata.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>

                <p>
                    Core records—datasets, projects and publications—have full landing pages. Supporting
                    records, such as people and organisational units, open in a drawer with its own navigation history.
                    Researchers can inspect related information without leaving the record they are evaluating or
                    losing the context of their investigation.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The difference between these behaviours also needed to be predictable. Navigation to a core record
                    uses a semantic link, while opening a drawer is an action and uses a button. Styling every action as
                    a conventional button would have overwhelmed pages containing many related records.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I retained the correct semantics in the markup and created a visual system for core-record links.
                    Each is accompanied by a record-type tag with a distinctive icon and label; the same tags appear in
                    search results and at the top of landing pages. Sighted users receive a consistent cue about the
                    destination, while screen-reader users encounter the semantic distinction between navigation and an
                    in-page action.
                </p>

                <figure class="case-study-figure record-relationships">
                    <!-- Suggested sources: a082f446-93cf-45c7-8067-daff497115e0.png and c7179300-d0d2-4d85-8a74-8dfba7101db0.png -->
                    <Image src={recordsRelationship} format="webp" quality={82}
                           alt=""
                           class=""/>
                    <div class="image-placeholder" aria-hidden="true">
                        Image placeholder: Supporting-record drawer beside a core-record link and its repeated
                        record-type tag.
                    </div>
                    <figcaption>
                        Caption placeholder: Contrast context-preserving supporting-record actions with navigation to
                        full core-record pages.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>Turning findings into a prioritised roadmap</h2>

                <p>
                    After the first implementation phase, I conducted a moderated UX evaluation with a researcher who
                    had not used MEx before. I observed them completing two realistic dataset-discovery tasks. The
                    session confirmed the iterative movement between datasets and variables and exposed issues across
                    metadata visibility, discoverability, language and search behaviour.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I documented each finding with a proposed response and assessed its expected impact. I then reviewed
                    the recommendations with the development team, estimated implementation effort and mapped them on
                    an impact–effort matrix. This gave us a shared basis for deciding what to deliver first.
                </p>

                <figure class="case-study-figure evaluation-priorities">
                    <!-- Suggested sources: 12e0450d-8068-465b-8afe-f9d434d436a2.png and a reconstruction based on 7cc26f1c-de6c-4eaa-ad9b-7eea54b78346.png -->
                    <!-- todo: make sure the arrows match the rest of the portfolio styles -->
                    <Image src={improvements} format="webp" quality={82}
                           alt=""
                           class=""/>
                    <div class="image-placeholder" aria-hidden="true">
                        Image placeholder: Improved search-result card paired with a simplified impact–effort matrix of
                        representative findings.
                    </div>
                    <figcaption>
                        Caption placeholder: Show how observed usability problems were translated into a prioritised
                        delivery roadmap without publishing the full internal report.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>

                <p>
                    Several high-impact, low-effort improvements were implemented first. Because the participant focused
                    on population, geographical and temporal coverage, we added this information directly to dataset
                    cards. A new control makes missing metadata explicit without overwhelming the default record view,
                    and we reduced the prominence of a global search that had been confused with the specialised
                    searches.
                </p>

                <p>
                    For the more complex search improvements, I led a workshop to translate broad expectations into
                    implementable matching and ranking rules—for example, whether a partial match in a title should rank
                    above an exact match in a description.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>Designing it—and making it real</h2>

                <p>
                    MEx is built on InvenioRDM, but its specialist metadata model and research journey required
                    substantial adaptation. My development background helped me recognise platform constraints early,
                    translate requirements into technical decisions and specify behaviour the team could build.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I annotated interaction behaviour, accessible labels, semantic HTML decisions and edge cases so
                    that the intended experience remained clear during development.
                </p>

                <figure class="case-study-figure design-to-implementation">
                    <!-- Suggested sources: 3c71be1c-ed54-4c1f-8a0f-7ea66832f0bf.png, a short code extract based on 3694fa99-044e-45a9-8e52-b7756283eeea.png, and a record-card crop. -->
                    <Image src={settings} format="webp" quality={82}
                           alt=""
                           class=""/>
                    <Image src={annotations} format="webp" quality={82}
                           alt=""
                           class=""/>
                    <div class="image-placeholder" aria-hidden="true">
                        Image placeholder: Annotated handover prototype, a short syntax-highlighted configuration
                        extract and the resulting rendered metadata card.
                    </div>
                    <figcaption>
                        Caption placeholder: Connect the design specification, maintainable configuration and final
                        implemented interface.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>

                <p>
                    I implemented all of the styling and the complete record-page interface. A colleague implemented
                    the search functionality from my designs, while other team members worked on the backend and
                    infrastructure.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I also translated the record-page design system into maintainable configuration. Card groupings,
                    labels, icons and display rules are defined centrally, allowing the interface to evolve alongside
                    the metadata model without duplicating decisions across templates.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>An ongoing partnership</h2>

                <p>
                    The features described here are implemented, and MEx continues to evolve. Before the initial
                    contract ended, RKI commissioned further development. At the end of 2025, the partnership continued
                    with a three-year agreement covering maintenance and new features. I remain involved as new
                    requirements and research needs emerge.
                </p>
            </section>

        </article>
    </main>
</BaseLayout>
